---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// A real page behind a real control. No modal, no waitlist form, no email capture —
// the honest thing to put behind an account button before accounts exist is a plain
// description of what the account will be, and the current status said outright.
const title = "Account | Basis";
const description =
  "How identity will work on Basis: a Telegram chat_id and nothing else — no email, no password, no wallet connection. What an account will store, what it will never store, and the current status.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Account</h1>
    <p>What an account here will be, what it will hold, and where it currently stands.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="empty" style="margin-bottom:28px">
    <div class="empty__icon" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10 6.5v4" /><path d="M10 13.4h.01" /><circle cx="10" cy="10" r="7.25" /></svg>
    </div>
    <div class="empty__body">
      <p class="empty__title">There are no accounts yet</p>
      <p class="empty__text">
        Nothing on this site requires signing in, and there is nothing to sign into. Accounts
        arrive with funding alerts, and not before — an account with nothing to deliver is a
        database of strangers, which is a liability rather than a feature.
      </p>
      <p class="empty__meta">Every page, number and tool here works without one.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section">How identity will work</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Alerts need somewhere to arrive. That delivery channel is the account — there is no second
    identity layered on top of it. You will message a Telegram bot; Telegram hands this site a
    numeric <code>chat_id</code>; that number is the account.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    That is the whole mechanism. There is no email address, no password, no password reset, no
    session cookie to steal, no OAuth provider, and no wallet connection or signature. A site
    that never accepts a password cannot leak one, and a site that never asks for a wallet
    signature cannot be phished for a wallet signature.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">What an account will store</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="max-width:720px">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Why it exists</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>chat_id</code></td><td>Where the alert is delivered. This is the entire identity.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Watched contracts</td><td>Which symbols you asked to be alerted about.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alert rules</td><td>The threshold and direction that trigger a message.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Timestamps</td><td>When a rule was set, and when it last fired, so you are not messaged twice.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section">What it will never store</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    No email address. No password or hash of one. No wallet address, and no connection to a
    wallet. No exchange API keys — this site reads public market data and has no reason to touch
    an exchange account, so it will never ask for the keys to one. No name, no phone number, no
    IP-based profile, and no third-party analytics tied to your identity.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Positions are not stored either. The <a href="/tools/liquidation-price">liquidation calculator</a>
    computes in the page from numbers you type; those numbers are not sent anywhere and not
    recorded. Your <a href="/watchlist">pinned contracts</a> are kept in this browser's local
    storage today and stay that way — an account is for delivering alerts, not for following you
    between devices.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Current status</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Not built. The alert pipeline it depends on is being assembled — funding snapshots are already
    being collected on a schedule, which is the same data an alert would fire from, and the
    <a href="/">sign-flip feed</a> on the homepage shows how far along that collection is. When
    alerts ship, this page becomes the place you connect Telegram, and this section says so
    instead.
  </p>
  <p class="section-note">
    Until then this button leads here and nowhere else. There is no waitlist to join and no email
    box to fill in, because there is nothing yet worth collecting an address for.
  </p>

  <style>
    h2.section { margin-top: 32px; }
  </style>
</Base>
